Chunk Overlap--

Given a text (or list of tokens), the goal is to split it into chunks of a fixed size while allowing for an overlap between consecutive chunks. The overlap is a specified number of tokens (or characters) that each chunk will share with its preceding chunk. This technique is particularly useful for embedding-based applications and vector database indexing where preserving context across chunk boundaries is essential.

Constraints:

Input: A string (or list of tokens).
Chunk size: An integer indicating the desired number of tokens per chunk.
Overlap: An integer indicating how many tokens each chunk should share with the previous chunk.
Edge conditions include ensuring that the overlap is strictly less than the chunk size and handling cases where the input may not fill a complete chunk.
Section 2 — Intuition
When splitting a text into chunks with overlap, the idea is to slide a window over the sequence. Each window (chunk) will start a fixed number of tokens ahead of the previous window’s start, where the step size equals the chunk size minus the overlap. This ensures that consecutive chunks share the overlapping words/tokens, which helps preserve context especially useful in downstream tasks like semantic search or information retrieval with vector databases.

Section 3 — Approach 1: Brute Force
A brute force approach can sequentially iterate over the input tokens and slice out each chunk while verifying indices.

Simply start at index 0 and in each iteration extract a chunk (current index to index + chunk_size).
Increment the start index by (chunk_size - overlap) each time until the end of tokens is reached.
Time Complexity: O(n) where n is the number of tokens; every token is visited once in slicing.
Space Complexity: O(n/chunk_size) for storing the resulting chunks list (the total number of chunks is proportional to n divided by the effective step size).

