Attaching Metadata to Every Chunk--

Attaching metadata to every chunk means that for every segment or piece of text (or any data object) you process into an embedding, you also include additional structured information (metadata). This metadata can contain information such as the source, document identifiers, timestamps, language, or any other relevant context that can later be used to filter or reassemble information.

2. Core Mechanism
The core idea behind attaching metadata is to ensure that every vector in your database isn’t just a raw embedding but also carries key context information. When performing similarity searches or filtering queries, this metadata plays a crucial role by allowing you to:

Narrow the search results based on attributes (e.g., date, topic).
Reconstruct the origin of the text or data.
Merge results across multiple data sources with different metadata tags.
3. Real-World Analogy
Consider a library where every book not only has an index but also a set of tags: genre, author, publication year, etc. These tags help a librarian (or a search engine) quickly locate the desired book based on specific criteria, even when multiple books share similar titles or topics.

